p.src:
A light ray in a medium with position-dependent speed $c(x,y)$ need not follow the shortest spatial route. The time required for a small segment is distance divided by local speed, so $T[y]
  =\int_{x_0}^{x_1}
  \frac{\sqrt{1+y'(x)^2}}{c(x,y)}\,dx.$ Fermat’s principle requires $\delta T=0.$ When $c$ is constant this reduces to the shortest-distance problem. In a variable refractive medium, however, the fastest path balances distance against local propagation speed.

