\begin{aligned}
  x&=X\cos\omega t+Y\sin\omega t,
  \notag\\
  y&=-X\sin\omega t+Y\cos\omega t.
  \end{aligned}

p.src:
The final term in Eq.  is linear in both coordinates and velocities. Applying the Euler–Lagrange equation gives $\begin{aligned}
  m\ddot X&=m\omega^2X-2m\omega\dot Y,
  \notag\\
  m\ddot Y&=m\omega^2Y+2m\omega\dot X.
  \end{aligned}$ The terms proportional to $\omega^2$ are centrifugal; those proportional to velocity are Coriolis. In vector form within the rotating plane, $\mathbf F_{\mathrm{C}}
  =-2m\,\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf v_{\mathrm{rot}},$ with signs adjusted if the opposite orientation convention is chosen.

